WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.919
HBO's Chernobyl purports to show not just
the causes of the historic nuclear accident,

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.020
but the bureaucracy that stood in the way
of rescue and recovery in the aftermath.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:13.460
With the real-life history being shrouded
in such secrecy, it's a story that can't help

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.280
but leave some questions unanswered.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:18.200
A lot of numbers get thrown around in Chernobyl.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:22.759
When Valery Legasov explains the worst case
scenario to the Soviet Central Committee,

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.080
we get figures of potential fatalities that
are downright catastrophic.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.780
When Legasov gives the numbers needed to contain
the disaster, from the cost it demands, to

00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:34.820
the years it will take, and the lives that
will be lost in the process, it sounds like

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:36.120
horrific science fiction.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:42.060
"For Byelorussia and the Ukraine, 'impact'
means completely uninhabitable for a minimum

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.300
of 100 years."

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:48.700
But one number audiences don't get is a concrete
death toll.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.880
Part of the problem is the Soviet Union's
lack of records relating to the topic, but

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:56.840
another, trickier issue is the way radiation
exposure actually causes death.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:01.039
While many people lost their lives in the
disaster's immediate aftermath, there were

00:01:01.039 --> 00:01:05.930
also untold numbers whose deaths were more
indirect, caused by radiation-related illnesses

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:07.320
that took years to develop.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:13.140
Notes at the end of the series put the death
toll between 4,000 and 93,000, but those numbers

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:14.940
are far from universally accepted.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:21.400
The US-based Union of Concerned Scientists
estimates between 4,000 and 27,000 lives lost,

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:26.140
while Greenpeace believes it to be in the
range of 93,000 to 200,000.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:30.340
Chernobyl showrunner Craig Mazin has also
shared estimates he's read that put the death

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:31.940
toll closer to a million.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.479
The estimates are so varied that unfortunately,
when it comes to the question of how many

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.220
people died from the Chernobyl disaster, the
only answer we can all agree on is "too many."

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:45.800
One of the most heartbreaking chapters in
Chernobyl comes in the fourth episode, in

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:51.060
which we follow a trio of Soviet liquidators
tasked with killing any animals, mostly domesticated

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.810
pets, left behind in abandoned villages.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:58.340
Fleeing civilians were not allowed to bring
pets with them for fear the pets could contaminate

00:01:58.340 --> 00:01:58.880
humans.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:03.299
While the episode was difficult for some viewers
to watch, showrunner Craig Mazin has said

00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:07.840
that his dramatized story is still a toned-down
version of what actually occurred.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.340
"They're radioactive, so they have to go.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:12.700
But it's not hard, they're mostly pets.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:14.420
They're happy to see you.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:15.600
They run right up to you.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:16.680
Bang."

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.580
But did the liquidators really manage to wipe
out all the pets who were left behind in the

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:21.620
evacuations?

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:22.540
Not quite.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:27.740
According to SPCA International and the Clean
Futures Fund, who frequently visit the Chernobyl

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:32.769
Exclusion Zone to provide spaying, neutering,
and vaccination services, over 800 dogs and

00:02:32.769 --> 00:02:37.730
cats still live in the area, subsisting off
of volunteer donations and their own foraging

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.040
skills to survive.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:44.760
In 2019, SPCA International announced that
for the first time, 15 dogs with radiation

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:49.160
levels low enough to be considered safe were
taken from the Exclusion Zone and adopted

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:50.560
by homes in the United States.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:55.940
Chernobyl's fourth episode opens with an elderly
woman milking a cow while a Soviet soldier

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:57.180
demands that she evacuate.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:01.680
The scene is inspired by true stories of people
who returned to their homes in the Exclusion

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.040
Zone in the years following the meltdown,
despite being legally barred from doing so.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:08.360
Amazingly, many of those people are still
there.

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:14.860
"Call them courageous, or just plain reckless,
Olga and Andrei Ruzhenko live here on a permanent

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:15.360
basis."

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:20.780
In 2017, the Irish Times visited the so-called
"samosely," or "self-settlers", those who

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:24.240
illegally returned to Chernobyl to live there
after the disaster.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:28.980
Despite warnings from scientists, the Times
said around 200 people still made their homes

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:31.260
there, some of whom were living well into
their eighties.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.820
But don't take their presence as evidence
that the Exclusion Zone is safe.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.480
While residents may not be dropping dead in
the grisly manners of Chernobyl's first responders,

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:45.020
cancer diagnoses, particularly thyroid cancer
and leukemia, have spiked since the accident.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:49.980
Despite these real dangers, the lives of the
self-settlers aren't nearly as post-apocalyptic

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:53.890
as you might think, and according to some
who've shared their stories with the media,

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:55.700
things where they live are just fine.

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:59.420
Though the residents aren't officially sanctioned
by the government, their presence is tolerated,

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:01.180
and they're given limited support.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:06.140
"The Ruzhenkos have been married 53 years,
and they say they don't need anything from

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:06.640
anyone."

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:12.140
The Chernobyl series opens two years after
the disaster, with Valery Legasov recording

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:14.000
his recollections on audio tapes.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:19.300
Before hanging , Legasov makes like
he's taking out the trash to fool his KGB

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:23.130
watchmen, and uses the opportunity to drop
the tapes in a nearby building.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:27.480
A note at the end of the series finale tells
us that Legasov's records were "circulated

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:31.820
among the Soviet scientific community," which
seems to confirm that this part of the story

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:32.320
is accurate.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:36.040
However, we never learn who Legasov expects
to retrieve the tapes.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:40.360
We don't know if he's been in contact with
a journalist, or perhaps with another scientist.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:45.020
It's not clear how his influential records
actually get out into the world.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:49.560
Speaking to The Cheat Sheet about how he prepared
for the role of Legasov, Jared Harris said

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:54.130
one of his biggest challenges was that there
was little historical record left on Legasov

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:54.640
to study.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:59.020
Just like it's threatened in the show, the
Soviet Union did indeed do a good deal to

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:00.340
erase him from history.

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:05.540
Harris also said he never listened to Legasov's
audio tapes, and he has a good reason; they

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:06.160
don't exist.

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:07.000
He explained,

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.720
"There were no audio tapes.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.200
He left behind journals.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:12.560
But that's not as cinematic as audio tapes."

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:18.100
Chernobyl's conclusion includes a 2006 quote
from Mikhail Gorbachev, the Soviet Union's

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:19.110
final leader.

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:23.870
It reads, "The nuclear meltdown at Chernobyl…was
perhaps the true cause of the collapse of

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:24.860
the Soviet Union."

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:28.640
But the series doesn't explore what exactly
that statement means.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:33.320
In 2013, Mark Joseph Stern wrote an article
for Slate explaining what he called the nuclear

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:35.460
theory of the USSR's collapse.

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:40.340
He explained that shortly before the explosion
at Chernobyl, Gorbachev had already introduced

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:45.620
the policy of glasnost, or openness of ideas,
expression, and institutional transparency.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:50.320
Stern wrote that it was Gorbachev's way of
combating the widespread practice of censoring

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.520
information and keeping secrets that the Chernobyl
series does such a good job of conveying.

00:05:55.180 --> 00:06:00.190
The policy of glasnost is widely credited
with fueling the USSR's eventual dissolution

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:01.120
in 1991.

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:05.820
The aftermath of the Chernobyl accident, Stern
argues, is what wound up fueling glasnost

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:06.420
the most.

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:11.640
Stern wrote that before Chernobyl, most Soviet
citizens, quote, "believed in the Soviet system,

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.080
forgave its flaws, and hoped for a better
future."

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:18.150
But the reactor explosion and everything that
came to light afterwards put questions in

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:22.940
the minds of citizens, crucial questions about
what was in their food and water, and whether

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:25.180
they should believe what the government was
telling them.

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:31.500
That new distrust for the Soviet state, according
to the theory, made the USSR's demise inevitable.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.880
The horrible aftermath of Chernobyl as depicted
in HBO's mini-series probably has plenty of

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:41.470
viewers wondering if what happened in 1986
Ukraine could happen again, in the United

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:42.500
States or elsewhere.

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:47.480
In 2016, Business Insider talked to Chernobyl
expert and author Andrew Leatherbarrow.

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:51.550
The writer and photographer took the opportunity
to explain why what happened at Chernobyl

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:53.640
simply couldn't happen in the US.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:55.480
The key, he says, is graphite.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:07:00.360
As the series makes clear in its final episode,
the power plant's graphite-tipped control

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:05.130
rods are what caused the reactor explosion,
accelerating the nuclear reaction instead

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:06.700
of helping to shut it down.

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:11.820
According to Leatherbarrow, using those rods
in the real life reactor was, quote, "an incredibly

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:12.940
foolish decision."

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:17.760
Thankfully, American reactors use water instead
of graphite to serve that function.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:20.350
As Leatherbarrow explained to Business Insider,

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:24.960
"There's absolutely no way the accident at
Chernobyl could have happened at any American

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:26.520
commercial power reactor."

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:30.660
But before you breathe easier, this doesn't
mean a meltdown can't happen at a nuclear

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:35.370
power plant in the U.S. Americans learned
a meltdown at one of their plants was possible

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:41.710
in 1979's Three Mile Island incident, just
as the Japanese learned in 2011 with the Fukushima

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:42.200
meltdown.

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:46.400
But the specific event that triggered the
Chernobyl accident couldn't happen in the

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:50.810
US, meaning the chance of any Americans having
to stumble around shovelling graphite off

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:53.900
of rooftops in the years to come is pretty
much close to zero.

